WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Lesson 6 is rich because it joins several ideas that students often meet as separate fragments.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.280
A transformer has a primary coil and a secondary coil.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.920
They are separate electrical circuits, but they are linked by changing magnetic flux in a shared core.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.560
Because the magnetic link is changing, the turns ratio sets the voltage change from primary to secondary in the school model.

5
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.200
And because power is ideally transferred across that link, a higher voltage usually corresponds to a lower current for the same power.

6
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.840
That is exactly why power transmission uses step-up transformers before long-distance lines.

7
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.480
This lesson stays long on purpose because the topic deserves more than one ratio sentence.

8
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.120
Keep this takeaway in view: A good transformer explanation begins with the magnetic link, then the turns ratio, then the grid consequence.

9
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.760
Physical link means changing core flux.

10
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.400
The primary and secondary are linked magnetically rather than by direct charge flow.

11
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:13.040
Voltage rule means turns ratio.

12
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.680
In the simple model, the voltage ratio follows the turns ratio.

13
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.748
This diagram matters because it shows all the important layers at once.

14
00:01:26.748 --> 00:01:33.816
The alternating current in the primary coil creates a changing magnetic flux in the core.

15
00:01:33.816 --> 00:01:40.884
That changing flux links the secondary coil and induces a voltage there.

16
00:01:40.884 --> 00:01:47.952
The number of turns in each coil then sets how large the secondary voltage is compared with the primary.

17
00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:55.020
The transmission panel extends that same story.

18
00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:02.088
If the voltage is stepped up for the same power, the line current falls, and resistive cable losses become smaller.

19
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:09.156
The diagram therefore turns the transformer from a memorized ratio machine into a physical system with a practical purpose.

20
00:02:09.156 --> 00:02:16.224
Keep this takeaway in view: The grid-bridge picture keeps the transformer physical, mathematical, and practical at the same time.

21
00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:23.292
Primary to core: Alternating current in the primary creates the changing magnetic flux.

22
00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:30.360
Core to secondary: The secondary receives its voltage by induction through the shared flux link.

23
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.359
A moving-belt analogy helps because it shows how two separate systems can be linked through a shared intermediary.

24
00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:42.358
One drum drives the belt, and the belt transmits the changing motion to another drum.

25
00:02:42.358 --> 00:02:48.357
The material of one drum does not leap across and become the material of the other.

26
00:02:48.357 --> 00:02:54.356
In a transformer, the shared changing magnetic flux plays the role of that belt.

27
00:02:54.356 --> 00:03:00.355
The primary circuit drives the changing magnetic link, and the secondary responds through induction.

28
00:03:00.355 --> 00:03:06.355
The analogy is useful because it keeps the circuits separate while still making the coupling feel real.

29
00:03:06.355 --> 00:03:12.354
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy keeps the circuits separate while making the magnetic coupling intuitive.

30
00:03:12.354 --> 00:03:18.353
Driver drum means like the primary.

31
00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:24.352
The primary provides the changing action that starts the shared-link story.

32
00:03:24.352 --> 00:03:30.351
Moving belt means like changing flux.

33
00:03:30.351 --> 00:03:36.350
The shared intermediary carries the changing effect across without merging the two systems.

34
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:42.835
Two relations matter in this lesson.

35
00:03:42.835 --> 00:03:49.319
The first is the turns-ratio rule: primary voltage divided by secondary voltage equals primary turns divided by secondary turns.

36
00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:55.804
That relation answers the voltage question.

37
00:03:55.804 --> 00:04:02.288
The second is the ideal power idea: primary voltage times primary current is approximately equal to secondary voltage times secondary current.

38
00:04:02.288 --> 00:04:08.773
That relation helps recover the missing current once the voltages are known.

39
00:04:08.773 --> 00:04:15.258
Together they explain why stepping voltage up tends to step current down for the same power.

40
00:04:15.258 --> 00:04:21.742
This is the mathematics behind the transmission argument.

41
00:04:21.742 --> 00:04:28.227
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 stays coherent when voltage ratio and power-current reasoning are used in the right order.

42
00:04:28.227 --> 00:04:34.712
Voltage question: Use the turns ratio when the missing quantity is a transformer voltage.

43
00:04:34.712 --> 00:04:41.196
Current question: After the voltages are known, use ideal power to recover the missing current.

44
00:04:41.196 --> 00:04:47.681
Vp / Vs = Np / Ns.

45
00:04:47.681 --> 00:04:54.165
Vp Ip ~= Vs Is.

46
00:04:54.165 --> 00:05:00.650
Use the turns ratio for voltage first, then use ideal power to infer the missing current.

47
00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:06.445
A dependable transformer routine has four moves.

48
00:05:06.445 --> 00:05:12.239
First name the magnetic link: changing flux in the core joins the two coils.

49
00:05:12.239 --> 00:05:18.034
Second use the turns ratio to find the missing voltage.

50
00:05:18.034 --> 00:05:23.828
Third use ideal power transfer to find the missing current if needed.

51
00:05:23.828 --> 00:05:29.623
Fourth explain the transmission consequence: higher line voltage means lower line current for the same power, so cable losses are reduced.

52
00:05:29.623 --> 00:05:35.417
This sequence matters because it stops the lesson from collapsing into a bare ratio exercise.

53
00:05:35.417 --> 00:05:41.212
Keep this takeaway in view: Link, then ratio, then power is the safest transformer reasoning routine.

54
00:05:41.212 --> 00:05:47.006
Step 1 means name the magnetic link.

55
00:05:47.006 --> 00:05:52.801
Start with induction through the shared core, not with symbols only.

56
00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:58.595
Step 2 means use the turns ratio.

57
00:05:58.595 --> 00:06:04.390
Work out the missing voltage from the turns relationship.

58
00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:10.639
A transformer needs a changing magnetic flux in the core, and that usually comes from an alternating current in the primary coil.

59
00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:16.888
A steady direct current would create a magnetic field, but after the transient moment it would not keep changing the flux in the way the secondary needs for continuous induction.

60
00:06:16.888 --> 00:06:23.137
This point is important because it reveals that transformers are not a separate electromagnetic world.

61
00:06:23.137 --> 00:06:29.386
They are an induction application built directly on the previous lesson.

62
00:06:29.386 --> 00:06:35.634
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformers need AC because transformers need continuous change.

63
00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:41.883
What is needed means changing flux.

64
00:06:41.883 --> 00:06:48.132
The secondary responds only when the magnetic link is changing.

65
00:06:48.132 --> 00:06:54.381
Why AC works means continuous change.

66
00:06:54.381 --> 00:07:00.630
Alternating current keeps the primary field and core flux changing.

67
00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:06.167
Students often picture a transformer as if charge simply travels from the primary wire into the secondary wire through the core.

68
00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:11.704
That is not the model.

69
00:07:11.704 --> 00:07:17.241
The two coils are separate circuits.

70
00:07:17.241 --> 00:07:22.778
The link between them is the changing magnetic flux in the shared core.

71
00:07:22.778 --> 00:07:28.315
Once that is clear, the voltage and current relations make much more sense, because the transformer is understood as a magnetic coupling device rather than as a hidden wire connection.

72
00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:33.852
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformers couple separate circuits magnetically, not by direct charge transfer.

73
00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:39.389
Primary circuit means separate loop.

74
00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:44.926
Charge moves in the primary loop only.

75
00:07:44.926 --> 00:07:50.463
Secondary circuit means separate loop.

76
00:07:50.463 --> 00:07:56.000
Charge moves in the secondary loop only after induction provides the voltage.

77
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.307
The transmission story is not decorative.

78
00:08:01.307 --> 00:08:06.613
It is the reason transformer mathematics matters in the power grid.

79
00:08:06.613 --> 00:08:11.920
Cable heating losses depend on the current in the line.

80
00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.227
If the same power is sent at a higher voltage, the current falls.

81
00:08:17.227 --> 00:08:22.533
And when the current falls, the I-squared-R heating loss in the cable drops dramatically.

82
00:08:22.533 --> 00:08:27.840
That is why power stations step voltage up for transmission and then step it down again near users.

83
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.147
Giving this its own scene keeps the practical logic visible.

84
00:08:33.147 --> 00:08:38.453
Keep this takeaway in view: Step-up transmission is about reducing line current and cutting cable losses.

85
00:08:38.453 --> 00:08:43.760
Same power means higher V means lower I.

86
00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:49.067
The power relation explains why voltage and current move oppositely when power stays fixed.

87
00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:54.373
Cable loss means depends on current.

88
00:08:54.373 --> 00:08:59.680
Lower current means less resistive heating in the transmission lines.

89
00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.537
Ask four checks.

90
00:09:03.537 --> 00:09:07.395
Are the primary and secondary directly joined by charge flow?

91
00:09:07.395 --> 00:09:11.252
No.

92
00:09:11.252 --> 00:09:15.109
Does the voltage ratio follow the turns ratio in the school model?

93
00:09:15.109 --> 00:09:18.967
Yes.

94
00:09:18.967 --> 00:09:22.824
For the same power, does a higher transmission voltage mean a lower line current?

95
00:09:22.824 --> 00:09:26.681
Yes.

96
00:09:26.681 --> 00:09:30.539
And is that lower current the reason transmission losses fall?

97
00:09:30.539 --> 00:09:34.396
Yes.

98
00:09:34.396 --> 00:09:38.253
Those checks show that the learner now owns the full transformer story, not just one ratio.

99
00:09:38.253 --> 00:09:42.111
Keep this takeaway in view: The quick check confirms that the final lesson is being read as one linked system.

100
00:09:42.111 --> 00:09:45.968
Link check means changing core flux.

101
00:09:45.968 --> 00:09:49.825
The two circuits communicate through induction, not through direct charge transfer.

102
00:09:49.825 --> 00:09:53.683
Ratio check means turns set voltage.

103
00:09:53.683 --> 00:09:57.540
The turns ratio is the clean first move for voltage questions.

104
00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:03.249
The worked example that follows is simplest if you enter it in stages.

105
00:10:03.249 --> 00:10:08.958
First remember that the transformer works by changing flux linking two separate coils.

106
00:10:08.958 --> 00:10:14.667
Next use the turns ratio to find the secondary voltage.

107
00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:20.376
Then use ideal power transfer to recover the missing primary current.

108
00:10:20.376 --> 00:10:26.085
That order keeps the problem physical, mathematical, and practical all at once, which is exactly what the final lesson is meant to achieve.

109
00:10:26.085 --> 00:10:31.795
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is easiest when mechanism, ratio, and power reasoning are used in that order.

110
00:10:31.795 --> 00:10:37.504
First move: State how the primary and secondary are linked by changing flux.

111
00:10:37.504 --> 00:10:43.213
Second move: Use the turns ratio to find the missing voltage.

112
00:10:43.213 --> 00:10:48.922
read the magnetic link first.

113
00:10:48.922 --> 00:10:54.631
Vp / Vs = Np / Ns.

114
00:10:54.631 --> 00:11:00.340
Use the ratio for voltage before using ideal power for current.
